{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q015_artifact_tag_not_homology","answer":"Verified SCOPe 2.08 facts: PDB 1a14 chain H is split into two domain records. d1a14h1 (sunid 20061, sccs b.1.1.1, region H:9-111) is the genuine protein domain: class b (All beta proteins) > fold b.1 (Immunoglobulin-like beta-sandwich) > superfamily b.1.1 (Immunoglobulin) > family b.1.1.1 (V set domains, antibody variable domain-like) > protein 'Immunoglobulin heavy chain variable domain, VH' > species Mouse (Mus musculus), cluster 3.2. d1a14h2 (sunid 343393, sccs l.1.1.1, region H:1-8) is NOT a protein domain in the evolutionary sense: its lineage is class l 'Artifacts' (sunid 310555) > fold l.1 'Tags' (310573) > superfamily l.1.1 'Tags' (310607) > family l.1.1.1 'Tags' (310682) > protein 'N-terminal Tags' (310894) > species 'Synthetic' (311501). So the second segment is an 8-residue N-terminal expression/purification tag of synthetic origin that SCOPe has deliberately separated from the VH domain so that it does not contaminate the Ig classification.

Should similarity to d1a14h2 be treated as homology evidence? No. The /homology endpoint for d1a14h2 returns homology_implied_by_level = false, homology_scope = null, and an explicit caution warning 'artifact_not_homology_evidence': 'Class l contains artificial sequence artifacts; similarity to these records does not imply biological homology.' Class l is a bookkeeping container, not a homology-bearing level: its 'fold', 'superfamily', and 'family' nodes are all simply named 'Tags' and group engineered sequences by their role in the construct, not by common ancestry or shared structural core. Therefore, unlike a shared b.1.1 superfamily (probable common ancestry) or a shared b.1.1.1 family (close relationship), sharing l.1.1.1 with d1a14h2 tells you only that another entry also carries a synthetic N-terminal tag. A BLAST or FATCAT hit covering only residues 1-8 of chain H should be discarded as artifact signal: it is not evidence of family, superfamily, or fold relationship, it must not be used to transfer function, species/taxonomy, or Ig-domain classification, and d1a14h2 must not be used as a structural template or representative for any biological protein. The annotations for d1a14h2 report no inconsistencies or repeats; the only comment ('complexed with ca, man, nag') describes the entry's ligands and does not change the artifact status.

Limits: SCOPe does not record the specific tag sequence or its purpose in the API responses retrieved, so the exact chemical identity of the tag (e.g., which expression construct) is not verified here; only its classification as a synthetic N-terminal tag artifact is. Any homology analysis of 1a14 chain H should be restricted to d1a14h1 (residues 9-111), and the class-l artifact warning should be applied to any other class-l hit as well.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1a14/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h2","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h2/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h2/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h2/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1a14h1/parents"]}]}
